		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://remembrance.sufipaths.net/?page_id=666#comment-9876</guid>
		<description><![CDATA[Dear Hayat had been a pupil of Inayat Khan in the 1920s. She told the story of returning to the room she shared with her sister after going to the silence session with Murshid as he opened his eyes to each of the mureeds in turn. Her sister was so frightened of the light in Hayat&#039;s eyes - saying over and over again, &quot;Your eyes, your eyes!&quot; 
For many years Hayat lived in Oakland, and held Sufi meetings.  She was deeply spiritual and dedicated her life to that work. Her library was astonishing, as she had inherited many esoteric books from older friends after they passed away. works of Manley P. Hall, L. Adams Beck&#039;s novels of a fictional 1920s mysterious esoteric Buddhist global network, and Olive Schriener&#039;s &quot;Dreams&quot;, to name a few. She loved birds, and as she worked for much of her life at Chapel of the Chimes, she would help save the birds that had been left behind. Her home was an oasis of peace in Oakland. Baby grand in the living room, kitchen facing the lovely back garden. A faint smell of burnt toast sometimes wafted through - her home was very near the crematorium and columbarium. She was completely comfortable with death and the spirit life. Her dining room chairs were from the Chapel of the Chimes. Even her pencils by the phone were from there, with printing on them: &quot;Crypts, Memorials...&quot; 
Hayat was invited to attend the Canadian Sufi Camp at Lake O&#039;Hara, where she shared with younger sufis many stories of her early days as a pupil of Inayat Khan.]]></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>Dear Hayat had been a pupil of Inayat Khan in the 1920s. She told the story of returning to the room she shared with her sister after going to the silence session with Murshid as he opened his eyes to each of the mureeds in turn. Her sister was so frightened of the light in Hayat&#8217;s eyes &#8211; saying over and over again, &#8220;Your eyes, your eyes!&#8221;<br />
For many years Hayat lived in Oakland, and held Sufi meetings.  She was deeply spiritual and dedicated her life to that work. Her library was astonishing, as she had inherited many esoteric books from older friends after they passed away. works of Manley P. Hall, L. Adams Beck&#8217;s novels of a fictional 1920s mysterious esoteric Buddhist global network, and Olive Schriener&#8217;s &#8220;Dreams&#8221;, to name a few. She loved birds, and as she worked for much of her life at Chapel of the Chimes, she would help save the birds that had been left behind. Her home was an oasis of peace in Oakland. Baby grand in the living room, kitchen facing the lovely back garden. A faint smell of burnt toast sometimes wafted through &#8211; her home was very near the crematorium and columbarium. She was completely comfortable with death and the spirit life. Her dining room chairs were from the Chapel of the Chimes. Even her pencils by the phone were from there, with printing on them: &#8220;Crypts, Memorials&#8230;&#8221;<br />
Hayat was invited to attend the Canadian Sufi Camp at Lake O&#8217;Hara, where she shared with younger sufis many stories of her early days as a pupil of Inayat Khan.</p>
